1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,350
The Curiosity Club.

2
00:00:02,350 --> 00:00:04,687
Episode 33.

3
00:00:04,687 --> 00:00:07,554
Breakfast Here, Bedtime There.

4
00:00:07,554 --> 00:00:10,271
Here is something to listen for.

5
00:00:10,271 --> 00:00:14,796
Why did Maya include two local times on the second invitation?

6
00:00:14,796 --> 00:00:16,222
Ready?

7
00:00:16,222 --> 00:00:18,301
Let us find out.

8
00:00:18,301 --> 00:00:22,309
Maya wants to call her cousin, who lives far away.

9
00:00:22,309 --> 00:00:27,282
She chooses a time after dinner and sends a message: Can we talk now?

10
00:00:27,282 --> 00:00:29,782
Her cousin replies later.

11
00:00:29,782 --> 00:00:31,385
Sorry.

12
00:00:31,385 --> 00:00:33,274
I was asleep.

13
00:00:33,274 --> 00:00:35,788
Pip checks Maya's clock.

14
00:00:35,788 --> 00:00:38,111
It was not bedtime here.

15
00:00:38,111 --> 00:00:41,766
Perhaps the cousin's clock needs new batteries.

16
00:00:41,766 --> 00:00:45,543
Leo brings out the globe from their day-and-night lesson.

17
00:00:45,543 --> 00:00:50,733
Different parts of Earth face the Sun at different times, he reminds them.

18
00:00:50,733 --> 00:00:56,222
Ms. Park explains that people use time zones to organize local clock time.

19
00:00:56,222 --> 00:01:02,989
When it is evening in one place, another place may be in the morning or even on a different calendar date.

20
00:01:02,989 --> 00:01:07,880
The club opens a world-clock display and chooses the actual cities.

21
00:01:07,880 --> 00:01:11,249
They do not guess the time difference from memory.

22
00:01:11,249 --> 00:01:16,915
Some places also change their clocks during part of the year, Ms. Park says.

23
00:01:16,915 --> 00:01:19,307
Checking the date matters.

24
00:01:19,307 --> 00:01:25,516
Maya writes two times on the invitation, one for each city, and includes the date.

25
00:01:25,516 --> 00:01:29,578
This time, her cousin joins while eating breakfast.

26
00:01:29,578 --> 00:01:32,948
I am having toast, the cousin says.

27
00:01:32,948 --> 00:01:36,508
I have already had dinner, Maya replies.

28
00:01:36,508 --> 00:01:39,198
Pip holds up an empty plate.

29
00:01:39,198 --> 00:01:43,043
I am available for any meal in any time zone.

30
00:01:43,043 --> 00:01:46,793
They laugh and compare the light outside their windows.

31
00:01:46,793 --> 00:01:55,747
After the call, Leo writes a planning rule: A good meeting time is a good time for everyone, not just the person sending the invitation.

32
00:01:55,747 --> 00:01:58,654
Now, a little more to discover.

33
00:01:58,654 --> 00:02:03,817
Time zones help people coordinate local clock times around the rotating Earth.

34
00:02:03,817 --> 00:02:08,206
Their boundaries follow human decisions as well as geography.

35
00:02:08,206 --> 00:02:12,499
Some places use seasonal clock changes and others do not.

36
00:02:12,499 --> 00:02:19,089
For a real call, checking both cities and the date is more reliable than assuming a fixed difference.

37
00:02:19,089 --> 00:02:21,929
Let us hear the main idea again.

38
00:02:21,929 --> 00:02:26,168
Maya's first invitation ignored her cousin's local time.

39
00:02:26,168 --> 00:02:30,978
The club checked a world clock and wrote both local times and the date.

40
00:02:30,978 --> 00:02:36,019
Breakfast and after-dinner conversation could then happen in the same call.

41
00:02:36,019 --> 00:02:38,994
Here are three useful expressions.

42
00:02:38,994 --> 00:02:42,119
You can listen, or say them with me.

43
00:02:42,119 --> 00:02:44,592
What time is it where you are?

44
00:02:44,592 --> 00:02:47,065
What time is it where you are?

45
00:02:47,065 --> 00:02:49,374
Does this time work for you?

46
00:02:49,374 --> 00:02:51,684
Does this time work for you?

47
00:02:51,684 --> 00:02:54,442
Let us check the date, too.

48
00:02:54,442 --> 00:02:57,201
Let us check the date, too.

49
00:02:57,201 --> 00:02:59,796
Remember our listening question?

50
00:02:59,796 --> 00:03:04,320
Why did Maya include two local times on the second invitation?

51
00:03:04,320 --> 00:03:06,453
Here is the answer.

52
00:03:06,453 --> 00:03:13,383
The callers lived in different time zones, so each needed to know when to join using their own local clock.

53
00:03:13,383 --> 00:03:17,038
And here is a question just for your imagination.

54
00:03:17,038 --> 00:03:21,861
What would you show a faraway friend during a short video tour of your neighborhood?

55
00:03:21,861 --> 00:03:24,972
There is more than one possible answer.

56
00:03:24,972 --> 00:03:28,220
Keep wondering, and see you next time.
